The '''Senate''' of [[Great Levantia]] was the central political organ of the state. It occupied a position of supreme influence over politics, the economy, and society during the republican period as the body through which the Levantine aristocracy debated matters of state. It only occasionally exercised direct legislative authority - a matter left to the [[Tribal Assembly (Great Levantia)|Tribal Assembly]] - but it maintained the right to veto any legislation brought to the Assembly, oversaw the conduct of Levantine elections, and maintained the {{wp|power of the purse}}. The office of Consul, which held executive power during the republican period, was almost always elected from among the Senate's number. After the rise of the [[Pontifex Maximus (Great Levantia)|Pontifex Maximus]], the Senate lost significant autonomy and power but remained an important source of political support for the [[Augustus (Great Levantia)|Augustus]]. The Senate was gradually rendered obsolete in the late Levantine period as the nobility of the [[Vigintiquinque|twenty-five tribes]] gained control over the voting processes in the Assembly. The rank and role of Senator continued a

{{Urcea WIP}}
