The '''Concordian Republic''' (''Res publica Concordianum'') was a successor state of [[Great Levantia]] which controlled the eastern half of the former province of [[Concordia]] in the 6th century. Not much is recorded about the state, and besides the ''[[Deeds of the Gaels]]'' it is mentioned only in four sources from the 6th century. Those sources say that the republic was established and governed by the Levantine legion stationed on the [[Limes Galaticus]] and that they still maintained the Limes at least as late as [[550]] AD. Concordia is chiefly referenced as an occasional ally of the [[Kingdom of Pumbria]], and eventually it was conquered by [[Gallawa]] by King Declan II in [[717]]. The ''Deeds'', written many centuries later, includes lengthy descriptions about men descended from the legions of [[Gaius Stephanus Pictor]] maintaining ancient Levantine traditions, including Levantine paganism, though these seem unlikely and are likely a post hoc rationalization of their conquest at the hands of the Gaels.

